00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
there's a new poll out this morning that  shows that labor is only four points  ahead of the conservatives in terms of  the public's voting intentions  why is that margin so close when the  conservatives are tearing lumps out of  each other in this leadership contest  well there'll be lots of different polls  that we can look at and of course  they've been going up and down let's not  forget that labour's had a 14-point lead  as well but what we've got actually  essentially is we've got two  conservative councillors as you say  taking lumps out of each other all the  while the state that we find the country  and is really as a result of uh their  decision-making in the last sort of  government that we've heard but we've  had the conservatives in power for 12  years i mean this week alone we saw that  the government is saying that it's going  to miss its own targets in terms of the  nhs waiting list we now have 6.6 million  people on the waiting list for planned  care i mean on that is the largest  number on record and you know the the  other thing as you say you know a basic  government functions are clearly not  working we've had the delays in terms of  uh lorry drivers i've had numerous  constituents contact me  as i'm sure mps across the country have  around uh delays in passport tens of  thousands of people are waiting over ten  weeks and we've also seen the court  backlog rise from 43  in the last five years and so yes of  course the conservatives have created  backlog britain and both of the  leadership candidates rishi and sunni  they're not talking about these issues  that are taking place they haven't got a  plan as to how they are going to  address this which is making life very  very difficult for lots of people um  and of course what the country needs is  labor i mean labor is the fresh thought  that britain deserves  well but but what i'm saying is that the  polls don't suggest that people back you  on that if the country really is in such  bad shape if it really is backlogged  britain and in the meantime we've got  this ferocious leadership race why isn't  labour romping ahead in every poll  well i think we are look there'll be  consistent polls weren't there and of  course when you have a leadership  election there'll be a bounce no doubt  for whoever wins for a short period of  time but let's not forget the country  hasn't forgotten i mean at the moment in  time nothing seems to be working and you  know there is crisis upon crisis and all  the while you know you've got two  candidates that are too bothered about  you know uh sort of appealing to their  sort of members as opposed to addressing  the cost of living crisis labor has put  forward lots of credible things actually  that could be done immediately to sort  of get uh inflation steady um cutting  bat for example the government just  hasn't got a plan i think it's just  unacceptable the leadership that's being  provided is actually being provided by  the labour party  well rishi sunak has promised to to cut  vat on energy bills and you talk about  the economy the bank of england is uh  expected to raise interest rates today  possibly by as much as half of one  percent so tell us what labour would do  to help vulnerable families  for whom that that could be a further  squeeze  well you can't just have tax cuts and  not have a plan for growth i mean we we  know that either that will mean that  you'll have public finances firing out  of control or you're going to have to  make cuts to public spending and neither  of those two options are something that  we can afford in this country  but as you say rising inflation is  really hitting so many families uh you  know very very hard but the low wage  spiral that we've actually seen is not  something new it's actually the result  of 12 years of the tory mismanagement of  our economy where living standards all  real wages have failed to absolutely  grow so taking bat off energy bills  would absolutely push inflation down  well this is something that labour has  been calling for and the government  could do that immediately it was steady  inflation as you say it will actually  put more money in people's pockets um  the very fact that we have low tax and  high growth uh economy is rather than  raising taxes rather than wasting  billions of pounds of taxpayers money as  this government has been doing they  should have been taking steps to boost  the economy and to grow the economy and  absolutely labor has a very credible  option in terms of making sure we have  an active industrial strategy with a  skill strategy that we focus on growing  those industries so we can create those  jobs of the future um  that is what it would require to grow  the economy  um we've just got a little bit of  breaking news actually that's just come  into us and offgem the energy regulator  has just confirmed that the energy price  cap is going to be confirmed it's going  to be updated quarterly rather than  every six months as happens at the  moment um it's warned that customers  face a very challenging winter ahead uh  something that we are of course aware of  so your response to that is it a good  move that offgem updates the price cap  every quarter rather than every six  months  i mean it's it's basically we know that  the prices are going to rise  significantly and they're going to  continue to go up so actually reporting  by two months is actually giving the  government uh you know a heads up to say  what is it that you are doing to  supporting people what what the  government shouldn't be doing is giving  tax breaks to oil and gas companies made  huge amounts of profits of four billion  pounds what labour is calling for is  that the government needs to be  supporting those families that are going  to be hit this winter those families are  really really struggling that many could  absolutely um support them in supporting  their families the government hasn't  just been able to come up with a  credible offer  i want to get your view on the situation  in taiwan you are shadow cabinet  minister for um  international development do you welcome  nancy pelosi's visit to taiwan  look i mean nancy pelosi's decision to  go and visit was a decision of hers and  of course she said this is about  solidarity and we've seen the response  from china being quite significant i  think we all want to see some dialogue  really want to see peaceful moves to  address the issues um across the  taiwan straits we've been very clear  about our serious concerns with china's  sort of increasingly aggressive action  uh towards taiwan and the attempts to  intimidate its democratic leaders but i  think the actions are really part of a  wider  pattern of china becoming much much more  assertive but the issue we have is that  we've got a government that's very  divided on how to actually approach  china it's got no clear strategy um and  you know both liz and rishi have  actually um had a record of chronic  inconsistency i would say around china  so we've got to absolutely be clear  we've got to be working with china we  know that it's important in terms of  trade um but you know we've got to also  uh support taiwan because you know on  issues of global health it is absolutely  right it has a seat on the table in  terms of the w health show uh and other  institutions  well how worried are you about the  fallout from nancy pelosi's visit and  and what china might do next and you  talk about supporting taiwan what form  should that take as far as labour is  concerned  well what labour has um you know what we  have said is that we will take a  strategic approach to china china in  terms of standing firm on our principles  and interests and managing the  differences that we have china we  recognize that of course we've got to do  trade with china we've got to work on  the big issues of climate of global  health uh we just can't you know do it  alone without their support so i think  that's important um in in terms of how  worried i am i think look let's wait and  see i mean you know it's his early days  i think the chinese also want to uh  reach out and and hopefully engage in  some form of talks i think that this can  be the opportunity to start to see if  that is very possible but let's wait and  see

